Nomenclature of Inorganic Chemistry — IUPAC Red Book and brief guide (@gc-red: 0, is #reference, #part-of @gc-sources) { r_0CW26ZVGP8AR7QH2KXCM36MB4J
Neil G. Connelly, Ture Damhus, Richard M. Hartshorn, and Alan T. Hutton, editors. Nomenclature of Inorganic Chemistry: IUPAC Recommendations 2005. RSC Publishing, 2005. ISBN 0-85404-438-8. [IUPAC's edition, official full PDF, corrections, brief guide, and translations](https://iupac.org/what-we-do/books/redbook/).

Begin with the linked Brief guide to the nomenclature of inorganic chemistry, Pure and Applied Chemistry 87, 1039–1049, 2015, DOI 10.1515/pac-2014-0718. Then read the book's relevant complete naming chapters, particularly composition, ions, oxidation states, acids, and coordination. Consult the published corrections and translation information rather than assuming every familiar classroom name is the preferred systematic one.

The official page supplies free authorized access, including a full PDF and a Portuguese-translation reference. Preserve actual publisher/IUPAC terms and component notices before adaptation; this trail links the source and writes its own explanations. Edition, editors, linked guide/corrections, and authorized availability checked on 29 October 2026.

